digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56941-4
  rankdir="TB";
  "C1" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given collective migration is important in major biological processes and valuable processes merit mechanistic study, therefore studying determinants/regulators of collective migration is valuable."];
  "C10" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given photoactivated adhesions activate PLD2 and increase PA and mTOR signaling and activated pathways can link membrane perturbation to behavior, therefore a membrane-tension-sensitive PLD2→PA→mTOR cascade plausibly transduces membrane mechanics into sustained behavioral change supporting collective migration."];
  "C12" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given membrane coupling without actin linkage yields collective migration and increased membrane tension, therefore increased membrane tension is plausibly sufficient and central for achieving collective migration under membrane-only coupling."];
  "C2" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given cadherin adhesions link to actin and signaling and cadherin perturbations confound membrane cues with cytoskeletal mechanotransduction, therefore standard cadherin perturbations cannot isolate membrane-derived signaling in collective migration."];
  "C3" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given membrane properties affect single-cell migration with sustained effects, therefore plasma-membrane-originated (possibly protein-independent) signaling cascades plausibly drive sustained changes in cell dynamics."];
  "C4" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given the contribution of plasma-membrane-derived signals to collective migration is unclear and tools are lacking, therefore a tool that isolates membrane cues is needed to test membrane-derived signaling in collective migration."];
  "C5" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given truncating cadherin cytoplasmic domains destroys adhesion and cadherin manipulations confound membrane versus cytoskeletal effects, therefore a non-cadherin engineered adhesion method is plausibly needed to create cell-cell coupling without canonical cadherin–actin linkage."];
  "C6" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Cph1-based photoswitchable adhesions induce cell-cell adhesion without actin linkage and such tools isolate plasma-membrane-originated effects, therefore Cph1-based photoswitchable adhesions are an appropriate tool to dissect membrane-based signaling in collective migration."];
  "C7" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given increasing cell-cell connections yields collective movement and adhesion strength determines collective versus individual migration, therefore experimentally increasing membrane-level cell-cell connections should be sufficient to switch otherwise single-cell migration into collective migration."];
  "C8" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given increased adhesions make cells faster despite friction expectations, therefore induced adhesions plausibly trigger active membrane-based mechanical/signaling changes that enhance motility."];
  "C9" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given photoactivated adhesions increase membrane tension and tension changes can mediate signaling, therefore membrane tension is plausibly a key mechanosensory mediator linking membrane coupling to downstream signaling that alters migration coordination."];
  "K1" [label="(0,0,0) If a process is important in morphogenesis/homeostasis/wound healing, then understanding its mechanistic determinants and controllable regulators is scientifically valuable."];
  "K10" [label="(0,0,0) If a tool can be controlled spatiotemporally (e.g., light-controlled), then it can dissect causal ordering between induced adhesion, membrane mechanics/signaling, and migration outcomes."];
  "K2" [label="(0,0,0) If cadherin adhesions link to the cytoskeleton and trigger signaling, then perturbations of cadherins usually confound membrane cues with cytoskeletal mechanotransduction and biochemical cascades."];
  "K3" [label="(0,0,0) If membrane properties affect single-cell migration and can produce sustained effects, then membrane-derived (including protein-independent) signals are plausible candidate regulators that could also influence collective migration."];
  "K4" [label="(0,0,0) If a causal contribution is unclear and appropriate tools are lacking, then a suitable research strategy is to develop/use a tool that isolates the suspected factor and then test its effect on the phenomenon of interest."];
  "K5" [label="(0,0,0) If an adhesion tool can induce cell-cell contact without actin linkage, then it can help isolate plasma-membrane-originated mechanical/signaling effects from adhesion-to-cytoskeleton mechanotransduction."];
  "K6" [label="(0,0,0) If increased adhesion is expected to increase friction and slow migration, but migration speeds up, then an additional active signaling mechanism (not just passive friction) is implicated."];
  "K7" [label="(0,0,0) If membrane tension increases upon induced membrane coupling, then membrane-tension-mediated signaling pathways are candidate mediators linking membrane coupling to altered cell migration dynamics."];
  "K8" [label="(0,0,0) If a pathway (e.g., PLD2→PA→mTOR) is activated downstream of the membrane perturbation, then that pathway is a plausible mechanistic link by which membrane-based signals can change migration behavior."];
  "K9" [label="(0,0,0) If membrane tension increase is sufficient to achieve collective migration, then selectively manipulating membrane coupling/tension is a valid method to test membrane-based signaling contributions to collective migration."];
  "KR" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given light-controlled tools can dissect causal ordering and Cph1 adhesions are light-controlled, therefore using light-controlled Cph1 adhesions can test causal links between induced adhesion, membrane mechanics/signaling, and migration outcomes."];
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, collective cell migration is a hallmark of morphogenesis, tissue homeostasis, and wound healing."];
  "N12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, although membrane mechanics regulate individual-cell dynamics, the contribution of plasma-membrane-derived signals to collective migration (independent of adhesion-to-cytoskeleton link and associated biochemical cascades) remains unclear."];
  "N13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, a key challenge is the lack of tools to dissect plasma-membrane mechanical cues from adhesion-molecule mechanotransduction to the cytoskeleton and biochemical signals."];
  "N14" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, truncating cadherin cytoplasmic catenin-binding domains makes E-cadherin nonfunctional because the membrane-anchored extracellular domain alone cannot sustain adhesion."];
  "N16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, artificial photoswitchable Cph1-based cell-cell adhesions can selectively elicit signals from within the plasma membrane; upon red light they induce adhesions without directly linking to actin."];
  "N17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, increasing cell-cell connections leads to coordinated collective movement in cells that otherwise migrate singly."];
  "N18" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, increased cell-cell adhesions did not slow cells as expected from added friction, but instead made them faster."];
  "N19" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, photoactivated increase in cell-cell adhesions increases membrane tension."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, the strength of intercellular adhesions determines whether cells migrate collectively or individually."];
  "N20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, photoactivated increase in cell-cell adhesions activates PLD2, increasing PA and activating mTOR signaling."];
  "N21" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, mechanical coupling at membranes via artificial adhesions lacking actin linkage induces increased membrane tension, which is sufficient to achieve collective migration."];
  "N3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, cadherin cell-cell adhesions link neighbors via extracellular domains, and α/β-catenin at the cytoplasmic tail forms a mechanosensitive link to the actin cytoskeleton."];
  "N8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, in single cells, changes in membrane properties significantly influence leading-edge dynamics and migration behavior."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The study proposes to use red-light-activated, membrane-tethered, cytoskeleton-uncoupled Cph1 adhesins to selectively increase cell-cell contacts and test whether this is sufficient to convert otherwise singly motile cells into a faster, coordinated collectively migrating cohort by raising plasma-membrane tension and activating PLD2/PA–mTOR signaling.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When a biological process is important but its membrane-derived mechanism is unclear, and an isolating light-controlled adhesion tool plus membrane-tension and PLD2/PA–mTOR readouts are available, the coherent research aim is to test whether engineered cell-cell contacts can causally drive collective migration through membrane-based signaling."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Because the contribution of plasma-membrane-derived signals to collective migration remains unclear, and Cph1-based photoswitchable adhesions can induce cell-cell contact without direct actin linkage, light-controlled Cph1 adhesions are a plausible strategy to test membrane-derived signaling contributions to collective migration."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Because membrane coupling without actin linkage is associated with increased membrane tension and collective migration, membrane coupling and plasma-membrane tension are plausible experimental levers for testing membrane-based signaling contributions to collective migration."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Because tools are needed to dissect plasma-membrane mechanical cues from adhesion-molecule mechanotransduction, the research plan should develop or use an isolating tool to test membrane-based signaling mechanisms in collective cell dynamics."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Using light-controlled Cph1-based artificial adhesions is an appropriate strategy to test causal links between induced cell-cell adhesion, plasma-membrane mechanics or signaling, and collective migration outcomes."];
  "K1" -> "C1" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "N1" -> "C1" [label="deduction-case"];
  "K2" -> "C2" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "N3" -> "C2" [label="deduction-case"];
  "N8" -> "C3" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
